  "body": "### What happens\n\nOn two Windows 11 machines (bacon: Intel NUC with iGPU; lenny: desktop), the Windows quick check of `main` is red on one step:\n\n```\nFAIL  ui suites: lib  exit 101\nprocess didn't exit successfully: `...\\camcraft_ui-b30d80154151484e.exe --test-threads=12` (exit code: 0xc0000005, STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION)\n```\n\nThe binary dies after about 100 tests with a native access violation, not a failed assert. Last tests printed before the crash (bacon, main `7a626a7f5`): the `egui_app::setup_plane_tests` / `setup_points_tests` group.\n\n### It is a concurrency crash\n\nThe same binary, same tree, run single-threaded on bacon:\n\n```\ncamcraft_ui-b30d80154151484e.exe --test-threads=1\ntest result: ok. 1425 passed; 0 failed; 7 ignored; finished in 166.04s\n```\n\nLinux (len, post-merge runners) and macOS (m3) are green on the same commits. Likely candidates: tests that create a graphics device/adapter (wgpu) or another thread-unsafe native resource in parallel. Related but different: #3258 (several UI binaries on one Linux host, fixed by #3302 naming the crash status); this one is one binary, several test threads, on Windows.\n\n### Workaround\n\n`--test-threads=1` for `camcraft-ui --lib` on Windows (the gate stays slower but green).\n\n**Done means:** `cargo xtask gate quick` on Windows runs `ui suites: lib` green at its normal thread count, or the gate serialises exactly the tests that share the native resource, with a test or gate-plan pin and the crashing resource named in the decision note.\n\n**Triage.** P3: a red gate on Windows only, with a workaround; no wrong output. `effort:medium`: test harness / gate plan, one crate.\n",
